"""Layer 15 MLP hidden unit 3782 (through this component), read as a 'necessity' feature.

The component is almost entirely MLP hidden unit 3782 of layer 15 (its input vector is
0.81 on that unit). Its activation is diffuse and context-dependent: most of its variance
is spread over many moderate values with no clean lexical driver. The part that has a
readable pattern is that it leans positive on words that announce a requirement or
obligation ("must", "need", "requires", "have to", "supposed to", "would"), most strongly on
the "to" that comes right after such a verb, and leans negative on negation and hedging
("not", "never", "wait", "maybe").

The program predicts that pattern: a small value for each cue, zero at the start of a
document and everywhere else. It explains only a little of the variance; see the
explanation for what it leaves out.
"""

READS: tuple[str, ...] = ()

# words that state a need or obligation, and the verbs after which "to" states one
NECESSITY = {"must", "need", "needs", "required", "require", "requires"}
TO_AFTER = {"have", "has", "had", "supposed", "need", "needs", "willing", "trying"}
CONDITIONAL = {"would"}
NEGATION_HEDGE = {"not", "never", "no", "n't", "wait", "maybe"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    t = prefix.position
    word = prefix.token_strings[-1].strip().lower()
    if t == 0 or prefix.document_ids[-1] != prefix.document_ids[-2]:
        return carry, 0.0
    prev = prefix.token_strings[-2].strip().lower()
    if word in NECESSITY:
        value = 0.5
    elif word == "to" and prev in TO_AFTER:
        value = 0.6
    elif word in CONDITIONAL:
        value = 0.3
    elif word in NEGATION_HEDGE:
        value = -0.15
    else:
        value = 0.0
    return carry, value

"""Layer 15 MLP neuron 3783 (this component's activation is almost exactly that neuron).

What it does, in plain terms: the neuron is mostly quiet, but it has a clear contrast/negation
flavour. Its big negative values come on the words that continue a negated or contrastive
phrase: "not [because] ...", "rather [than] ...", "due [to] ...", "not [a] lack of ...",
and on words like "actually / really / real / genuinely", which it pushes the output toward.
Its positive values come on the words that follow a negation in "not only", "not just",
"not enough", "not as". Everything else sits near zero.

This program tracks whether a negation word has appeared earlier in the same clause (the
clause ends at punctuation, and resets at each new document), and predicts:
  - a negative value on a word that is inside such a clause and is one of the contrast
    continuations (because, than, a, the, of, to, ...),
  - a strong negative value on the truth-adverbs (actually, really, real, ...),
  - a positive value on "only / just / enough / even" right after a negation.
It explains only a small share of the variance: most of this neuron's variance sits in
rare spikes whose context is not captured by words alone.

Main program: `step` reads the newest token and the previous token, updates the clause state,
and returns the weighted sum of the rules above.
"""

READS = ()

NEGATIONS = {"not", "n't", "’t", "no", "never", "nor", "neither", "rather", "without", "cannot"}
CONTRAST = {
    "because", "than", "a", "an", "the", "of", "to", "from", "by", "that", "as", "due",
    "or", "lack", "any", "simply",
}
ONLY = {"only", "just", "enough", "even", "both", "all", "too"}
TRUTH = {
    "actually", "really", "actual", "real", "genuinely", "genuine", "truly", "directly",
    "direct", "necessarily", "explicitly", "inherently", "literally",
}


def _word(token_string):
    return token_string.strip().lower()


def _is_boundary(token_string):
    text = token_string.strip()
    return bool(text) and not any(c.isalnum() for c in text) and text not in {"'", "’"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    last_doc, negated = carry if carry is not None else (None, False)
    doc = prefix.document_ids[-1]
    if doc != last_doc:
        negated = False  # a new document starts a fresh clause
    word = _word(prefix.token_strings[-1])
    prev = _word(prefix.token_strings[-2]) if len(prefix.token_strings) > 1 and doc == last_doc else ""

    # Value at this position: the clause state before this word, plus the word itself.
    value = 0.0
    if negated:
        value -= 0.12
        if word in CONTRAST:
            value -= 0.26
        if word in ONLY:
            value += 0.33
    if prev in NEGATIONS:
        if word in ONLY:
            value += 1.13
        if word in CONTRAST:
            value -= 1.54
    if word in TRUTH:
        value -= 1.35

    # Update the clause state for the next position.
    if _is_boundary(prefix.token_strings[-1]):
        negated = False
    if word in NEGATIONS:
        negated = True
    return (doc, negated), value

"""Layer 15, MLP down-projection component 3784: a weak word-class predictor.

What the component is: a signed, diffuse feature of the residual stream. Its read-out (the
unembedding of its write direction) pushes the next token toward word-piece continuations
and away from capitalised word starts. Its activation is not explained by any simple
feature; the classes below only capture a small part of it.

What the program predicts, from the current token alone, the mean activation of each class:
- a phrase-introducing word after a space (a preposition or subordinator such as "after",
  "during", "because", "based", "due", "according"): clearly positive;
- any other word after a space: mildly positive;
- a fragment inside a word (letters with no leading space, e.g. "ly" in "ear-ly"): negative;
- a Chinese character: mildly positive;
- anything else (punctuation, digits, symbols, empty or partial bytes): zero.
"""

READS = ()

PHRASE_WORDS = set(
    "about across after against along among around at before behind below between by "
    "despite during for from in into like near of off on over through throughout till to "
    "toward towards under until upon via with within without because although while if "
    "so that due based according depending concerning regarding following including "
    "containing consists compared instead rather".split()
)


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    s = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    word = s.strip().lower()
    if not word:
        return carry, 0.0
    if word.isascii() and word.isalpha():
        if s.startswith(" "):
            value = 0.45 if word in PHRASE_WORDS else 0.12
        else:
            value = -0.3
    elif "一" <= word[0] <= "鿿":
        value = 0.27
    else:
        value = 0.0
    return carry, value

"""Plurality and 'other' words: a signed, mostly negative response on quantity words.

What it does. This component's output, read through the unembedding, favours words like
"both", "each other", "respectively" when its activation is negative. Its activation is
therefore most negative on quantity words that open a generic multi-item phrase: "run
multiple commands", "there are two barriers", "a plurality of", "a series of". It is
near zero or positive on the same words after a definite marker: "the two", "these",
"those", and on "of" after a specific plural noun ("types of", "pair of").

How the program reads it. Each word group has a base value, a shift when the previous
word is a definite marker, and a shift when it is a verb or preposition that introduces a
new referent. Activations elsewhere are predicted as 0. The values are small
least-squares fits, shrunk toward 0, since larger guesses predicted worse.
"""

READS = ()

# group: (words, base, shift after a definite marker, shift after an indefinite cue)
GROUPS = [
    (("multiple",), -2.4, 2.2, -1.7),
    (("two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "twice", "double"), -0.4, 1.0, -0.5),
    (("other", "another", "different", "separate", "distinct", "additional", "second"), -0.6, 0.0, 0.0),
    (("these", "those"), 0.5, 0.0, 0.0),
]
DEFINITE = {"the", "these", "those", "first", "last"}
INDEFINITE = {"create", "run", "use", "if", "when", "having", "where", "into", "with", "between",
              "by", "from", "at", "in", "for", "of", "are", "there", "have", "has"}
OF_SPECIFIC = {"types", "kinds", "classes", "methods", "forms", "pairs", "pair", "sets", "groups", "values"}
OF_GENERIC = {"plurality", "series", "combination", "variety", "sum", "dozens", "hundreds", "number", "array"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    word = prefix.token_strings[-1].strip().lower()
    if len(prefix.token_ids) < 2 or prefix.document_ids[-2] != prefix.document_ids[-1]:
        before = ""
    else:
        before = prefix.token_strings[-2].strip().lower()
    if word == "of" and before:
        if before in OF_SPECIFIC:
            return carry, 0.7
        if before in OF_GENERIC:
            return carry, -0.5
        return carry, 0.0
    for words, base, after_def, after_ind in GROUPS:
        if word in words:
            value = base
            if before in DEFINITE:
                value += after_def
            elif before in INDEFINITE:
                value += after_ind
            return carry, value
    return carry, 0.0

"""Optional-offer detector for layers.15.mlp.down_proj:3801.

The component goes strongly negative on words that present an optional or
alternative course of action to the reader: "if you need ...", "feel free to ...",
"if desired", "you can ... / or you can ...", "also available", "optional".
Elsewhere it is near zero with a diffuse, hard-to-predict spread.

This program does not reproduce the rare large events exactly. It assigns each
word a fixed value (group means measured on training text), negative for words in
an optional-offer context and near zero otherwise.
"""
READS = ()

# Words that express wanting or needing something (usually inside "if you want/need ...").
WANT = {"want", "wants", "wanted", "wish", "wished", "need", "needs", "needed", "desire", "desired"}
# Words that mark something as optional, free, available or possible.
OPTION = {"free", "optional", "option", "available", "interested", "ready", "possible", "necessary", "required"}
# Modals that offer a permitted action.
MODAL = {"can", "could", "may", "might", "also", "too"}
# Conditional openers.
COND = {"if", "when", "whenever", "unless"}
# Words just before a trigger that make it an offer to the reader ("you can", "or you can", "feel free").
OFFER_CONTEXT = {"you", "feel", "if", "anyone", "who", "they", "we", "or", "but", "also", "can", "may"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    word = prefix.token_strings[-1].strip().lower()
    before = prefix.token_strings[-2].strip().lower() if len(prefix.token_strings) > 1 else ""
    offer = before in OFFER_CONTEXT
    if word in OPTION:
        value = -2.5 if offer else -0.5
    elif word in WANT:
        value = -1.0 if offer else -0.2
    elif word in MODAL:
        value = -0.9 if offer else -0.3
    elif word in COND:
        value = -0.45 if offer else -0.27
    else:
        value = 0.0
    return carry, float(value)

"""Layer 15 MLP down-projection component 3811: a sparse negative response on words that
introduce what something changes into, or where it goes, right after a verb of change.

Most of its activation is near zero (std about 0.3), with heavy tails. The clearest
responses are negative: "become", "now" and "into" (the change words), and "to", "by",
"with", "as" directly after a verb of change or transfer ("has become", "turned into",
"traded to", "multiplied by", "elevated to"). Nothing else in the prefix separates the
strong positive responses, so the program predicts only these negative means and 0
elsewhere.
"""

READS = ()

CHANGE_WORDS = {"become", "becomes", "became", "becoming", "now", "into"}
VERBS_OF_CHANGE = {
    "become", "becomes", "became", "turn", "turns", "turned", "transform", "transformed",
    "convert", "converted", "elevate", "elevated", "move", "moved", "trade", "traded",
    "transfer", "transferred", "multiply", "multiplied", "change", "changed", "extend",
    "extended", "adapt", "adapted",
}
PREPOSITIONS = {"to", "by", "with", "as"}
CHANGE_WORD_MEAN = -0.28  # measured mean activation on these words
AFTER_CHANGE_VERB_MEAN = -0.68  # measured mean on a preposition after a verb of change


def word(text):
    return text.strip().lower()


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    if len(prefix.token_strings) < 2 or prefix.document_ids[-1] != prefix.document_ids[-2]:
        return None, 0.0
    cur, prev = word(prefix.token_strings[-1]), word(prefix.token_strings[-2])
    if cur in PREPOSITIONS and prev in VERBS_OF_CHANGE:
        return None, AFTER_CHANGE_VERB_MEAN
    if cur in CHANGE_WORDS:
        return None, CHANGE_WORD_MEAN
    return None, 0.0

"""Layer 15 MLP hidden unit 3815 (component p-82af68dd ... down_proj:3815), read as a
"parallel options / contrast" detector.

This component is, in effect, one hidden unit of the layer-15 MLP: its input direction is
dominated by that unit, and its output direction (through the tied embedding) promotes
contrast and pairing connectives ("whereas", "conversely", "meanwhile", "相反", "反之") and
suppresses additive ones ("also", "extend", "addition"). Its strongest positive values come
where the text has set up two parallel options and the next word is the second half of the
pair: after "both", "either", "whether", "vs", "while", or the "or"/"and" that completes such
a pair ("benefits of both gun-up [and]", "either solid [or]", "one way [or] the other").

The rule below gives small values for those cues and small negative values for list-like
or code-like tokens. It is a readable description of what the unit responds to. It explains
almost none of the variance: most of the unit's activation is a diffuse, context-dependent
bulk (token identity alone explains about 1%), which no short rule captures.
"""

from collections.abc import Mapping

READS: tuple[str, ...] = ()

OPENERS = {"both", "either", "neither", "whether", "between", "vs", "versus"}
PAIR_CLOSERS = {"or", "and", "nor", "but", "than", "while", "whereas"}
CONTRAST_WORDS = {
    "whereas", "while", "conversely", "however", "but", "instead", "meanwhile",
    "vs", "hand", "alternatively", "although",
}
LIST_WORDS = {"other", "second", "one", "each", "than", "both"}
CODE_SYMBOLS = set("=(){}[]$`")
WINDOW = 8  # how far back a pair opener still counts


def step(carry, prefix, upstream: Mapping):
    word = prefix.token_strings[-1].strip().lower()
    doc = prefix.document_ids[-1]
    # the last few words, within the same document only
    recent = []
    for i in range(max(0, len(prefix.token_strings) - WINDOW - 1), len(prefix.token_strings) - 1):
        if prefix.document_ids[i] == doc:
            recent.append(prefix.token_strings[i].strip().lower())
    value = 0.0
    if word in OPENERS:
        value += 0.15
    if word in PAIR_CLOSERS and any(w in OPENERS for w in recent):
        value += 0.2
    if word in CONTRAST_WORDS:
        value += 0.28
    if word in LIST_WORDS:
        value -= 0.08
    if word and all(c in CODE_SYMBOLS for c in word):
        value -= 0.06
    return carry, float(value)

"""Layer 15, MLP hidden unit 3817 (the down_proj component 3817), as a readable rule.

Almost all of this component's input is one MLP hidden unit (layer 15, unit 3817), so
the component's activation is that unit's value times a fixed scale. The unit is
negative on calendar-period words used as relative or recurring time references:
"every day", "each morning", "this week", "next year", "the summer". It is weaker on
other time words ("today", "school", "past") and stays near zero for counted spans such
as "two weeks" or "3 days". Words about being absent, ill or interrupted ("sick",
"missed", "unable", "cancelled", "pandemic") push it positive.

The rule below predicts those tendencies and is zero elsewhere. It explains almost none
of the variance: most of the unit's activity is a diffuse +/-0.4 that no word list
captures, and the time and absence words carry only a few percent of its energy.
"""
from collections.abc import Mapping

READS: tuple[str, ...] = ()

TIME_WORDS = {
    "day", "days", "week", "weeks", "year", "years", "month", "months", "season",
    "seasons", "morning", "night", "evening", "weekend", "summer", "winter", "spring",
    "autumn", "semester", "birthday", "christmas", "today", "tonight", "daily",
    "weekly", "monthly", "annual", "yearly", "breakfast", "dinner", "tomorrow",
    "yesterday",
}
COUNTS = {"one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten",
          "several", "many", "few"}
RECURRING = {"every", "each", "per"}
DETERMINERS = {"this", "next", "last", "my", "our", "your", "his", "her", "their", "all"}
ABSENCE = {"sick", "ill", "illness", "sickness", "missed", "missing", "unable", "absence",
           "absent", "cancelled", "canceled", "covid", "pandemic", "lockdown", "flu",
           "excuse", "injury"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream: Mapping):
    word = prefix.token_strings[-1].strip().lower()
    same_doc = len(prefix.document_ids) > 1 and prefix.document_ids[-2] == prefix.document_ids[-1]
    prev = prefix.token_strings[-2].strip().lower() if same_doc else ""
    if word in TIME_WORDS:
        if prev in COUNTS:
            value = 0.0
        elif prev in RECURRING:
            value = -1.1
        elif prev in DETERMINERS:
            value = -0.9
        else:
            value = -0.3
    elif word in ABSENCE:
        value = 0.6
    else:
        value = 0.0
    return carry, value

"""Component layers.15.mlp.down_proj:3826 (a diffuse feature with a few strong triggers).

In plain terms, this component fires most strongly on the words that name a tool or
its action inside an agent's tool call, e.g. the "ash" of {"name": "execute_bash"},
the "_replace" and "_editor" of {"name": "str_replace_editor"}, and "view", "create",
"execute", "submit". While the model is inside a <tool_call> ... </tool_call> block,
those words get a large value (18), and the looser command words "str", "cd", "cat",
"grep", "run", "path" get a smaller one (6).
Outside tool calls it is weak: it leans positive on the word "cat" (as in `cat -n`
output), on <|endoftext|>, and on property names of knowledge-base style facts
("country", "instance of", "sports team", "occupation", "educated at", ...), and leans
slightly negative on chat-role and tool-response markers. Everything else is near zero.

The program tracks only whether it is inside a tool call (from the newest token, reset
at each document start) and reads the newest token. It explains the strong, sparse
part of the component; the rest of its variance is diffuse and is not modelled here.
"""

READS = ()

# Words that name a tool or its action: strong inside a tool call.
TOOL_WORDS = {"bash", "ash", "execute", "view", "_replace", "_editor", "create", "submit"}
# Looser command or argument words: weaker inside a tool call.
COMMAND_WORDS = {"str", "cd", "cat", "grep", "run", "finish", "_range", "_text", "_b", "path"}
# Property names of knowledge-base style facts ("X — country — Y").
PROPERTY_WORDS = {
    " country", " instance", " sports", " member", " occupation", " educated",
    " award", " spoken", " located", " genre", " performer", " team", " cast", " sport",
}
# Chat-role and tool-response markers, weakly negative.
MARKER_WORDS = {"assistant", "user", "<tool_response>", "<|im_start|>"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    # carry: whether the previous token left us inside a <tool_call> block.
    inside = bool(carry)
    if len(prefix.document_ids) > 1 and prefix.document_ids[-1] != prefix.document_ids[-2]:
        inside = False  # a new document starts: no tool call is open
    tok = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    if tok == "<tool_call>":
        inside = True
    elif tok == "</tool_call>":
        inside = False

    if inside:
        if tok in TOOL_WORDS:
            value = 18.0
        elif tok in COMMAND_WORDS:
            value = 6.0
        elif tok == '",':
            value = 4.0
        else:
            value = 0.0
    elif tok == "cat":
        value = 9.0
    elif tok == "<|endoftext|>":
        value = 2.3
    elif tok in PROPERTY_WORDS:
        value = 3.0
    elif tok in MARKER_WORDS:
        value = -2.0
    else:
        value = 0.0
    return inside, value
